{"entity":{"id":"cs:563","name":"Transvaal","dataset":"cshapes","kind":"polity"},"territories":[{"id":10500,"name":"Transvaal","set_id":"cs","class":"republic","valid_from":1886,"valid_to":1910.4082191780822,"snapshot_held":0,"area_km2":301824,"border_precision":3,"capital_name":"Pretoria","sovereign":null,"sovereign_note":null,"modern_name":"South Africa","min_lon":24.779212,"min_lat":-28.648509,"max_lon":32.034467,"max_lat":-22.136391,"source_file":"CShapes-2.0.geojson","source_row":"gwcode=563;start=1886-1-1"}],"identities":[{"qid":"Q550374","from_t":1886,"to_t":1902.4109589041095,"method":"name-template+date","confidence":0.8952400382934257,"label":"South African Republic","description":"independent Boer-ruled country in Southern Africa between 1856–1902","inception":1852.0437158469945,"dissolved":1902.4109589041095,"enwiki":"South African Republic","summary":"The South African Republic (Dutch: Zuid-Afrikaansche Republiek, abbreviated ZAR; Afrikaans: Suid-Afrikaanse Republiek), also known as the Transvaal Republic, was a landlocked independent Boer republic in Southern Africa which existed from 1852 to 1902, when it was annexed into the British Empire as a result of the Second Boer War.\nThe ZAR was established as a result of the 1852 Sand River Convention, in which the British government agreed to formally recognise the independence of the Boers living north of the Vaal River. Relations between the ZAR and Britain started to deteriorate after the British Cape Colony expanded into the Southern African interior, eventually leading to the outbreak of the First Boer War between the two nations. The Boer victory confirmed the ZAR's independence; however, Anglo-ZAR tensions soon flared up again over various diplomatic issues."},{"qid":"Q1187978","from_t":1902.4109589041095,"to_t":1910.4082191780822,"method":"name-alias+date","confidence":0.8319908350169151,"label":"Transvaal Colony","description":"British colony from 1877 to 1881 and 1902 to 1910","inception":1902,"dissolved":1910,"enwiki":"Transvaal Colony","summary":"The Transvaal Colony (Afrikaans pronunciation: [transˈfɑːl]) was the name used to refer to the Transvaal region during the period of direct British rule and military occupation between the end of the Second Boer War in 1902, when the South African Republic was dissolved, and the establishment of the Union of South Africa in 1910. The borders of the Transvaal Colony were larger than the defeated South African Republic (which had existed from 1852 to 1902). In 1910 the entire territory became the Transvaal Province of the Union of South Africa."}],"facts":[{"qid":"Q550374","kind":"capital","value_qid":"Q3926","value_label":"Pretoria","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P36","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Pretoria"},{"qid":"Q550374","kind":"official_language","value_qid":"Q7411","value_label":"Dutch","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P37","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Dutch"},{"qid":"Q550374","kind":"government","value_qid":"Q7270","value_label":"republic","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P122","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"republic"},{"qid":"Q550374","kind":"predecessor","value_qid":"Q1493843","value_label":"Klein Vrystaat","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1365","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Klein Vrystaat"},{"qid":"Q550374","kind":"successor","value_qid":"Q1187978","value_label":"Transvaal Colony","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P156","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Transvaal Colony"},{"qid":"Q550374","kind":"flag","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Flag of Transvaal.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P41","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q550374","kind":"coat","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Coat of arms of the South African Republic.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P94","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q550374","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q462674","value_label":"